      "text": " light-sensitive molecules within plants, which convert the sun's rays to their own energy, respond in the same way plants do.\n\nBecause these small molecules, called prokaryotes, store an energy of up to 20 times that of plants' own energy, it creates plants with an even bigger energy reserve and more energy needs per cubic meter of air than plants with lower or no indoor air pressure. These large molecules also reduce the amount of oxygen that can be released.\n\nIn his report, Wang found that this energy reserve is higher than plants' own on many fronts. Plants with less oxygen in the air supply less carbon dioxide into the environment as they breathe, thus allowing more air to air-depose plants and reduce greenhouse gas emissions.\n\nIn effect, plants are burning carbon dioxide to use up all their energy during their natural, high-intensity flowering. This leaves them with less air to breathe because of this high amount of air that they take in.\n\n\"",
